1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:03,590
There are four ways to waste
the last week before the SAT.

2
00:00:03,610 --> 00:00:04,968
Cramming new topics.

3
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:07,040
Changing your sleep.

4
00:00:07,432 --> 00:00:10,147
Taking another full test
in the last two days.

5
00:00:10,790 --> 00:00:13,270
And the fourth feels like real studying,

6
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:15,004
which is why it's easy to miss.

7
00:00:15,062 --> 00:00:17,241
The first three are easy to avoid.

8
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:20,312
The fourth is drilling without
knowing why you missed.

9
00:00:20,812 --> 00:00:24,277
Day six is where you
fix it, so stay for it.

10
00:00:25,277 --> 00:00:29,670
The rule all week: review, don't cram.

11
00:00:30,527 --> 00:00:35,062
Here's the plan, day by day, from one
week out to the moment you walk in.

12
00:00:35,098 --> 00:00:36,098
Day seven.

13
00:00:37,597 --> 00:00:38,685
One week out.

14
00:00:38,705 --> 00:00:40,935
Take a full-length
practice test in Bluebook,

15
00:00:40,955 --> 00:00:43,597
the same app you'll use on test day.

16
00:00:43,633 --> 00:00:46,884
The practice tests there are
timed like the real thing,

17
00:00:47,169 --> 00:00:51,542
and College Board recommends taking at
least one with the timer on and breaks

18
00:00:51,562 --> 00:00:52,598
like test day.

19
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:54,859
Make it feel like test day.

20
00:00:55,073 --> 00:00:56,696
Start in the morning.

21
00:00:56,716 --> 00:00:58,966
Sit at a desk, not on your bed.

22
00:00:59,216 --> 00:01:01,002
Put your phone in another room.

23
00:01:01,215 --> 00:01:03,108
Use real scratch paper.

24
00:01:03,287 --> 00:01:07,323
And take only the ten-minute break
between Reading and Writing and Math.

25
00:01:07,404 --> 00:01:10,599
Don't stop to look things up,
and don't pause the clock.

26
00:01:10,619 --> 00:01:12,706
The point isn't just the score.

27
00:01:12,726 --> 00:01:15,705
It's to find out what goes
wrong under real timing,

28
00:01:15,725 --> 00:01:17,511
so the rest of the week can fix it.

29
00:01:17,725 --> 00:01:21,475
And starting today, wake up at the
time you'll wake up on test day.

30
00:01:21,507 --> 00:01:22,507
Day six.

31
00:01:23,078 --> 00:01:24,363
Review the test.

32
00:01:25,614 --> 00:01:29,237
After a Bluebook practice test,
your scores show up in My Practice,

33
00:01:29,257 --> 00:01:32,077
where you can review each
question with its explanation.

34
00:01:32,328 --> 00:01:33,915
Go through every question you missed,

35
00:01:33,935 --> 00:01:36,900
and every question you guessed
on, even if you got it right.

36
00:01:36,983 --> 00:01:40,804
For each one, don't just read
the explanation and move on.

37
00:01:41,054 --> 00:01:42,983
Write down why you missed it.

38
00:01:43,197 --> 00:01:46,197
Almost every miss fits one of four labels.

39
00:01:46,255 --> 00:01:48,148
One, content.

40
00:01:48,862 --> 00:01:50,827
You didn't know the rule or the formula.

41
00:01:51,434 --> 00:01:53,255
Two, careless.

42
00:01:53,648 --> 00:01:57,255
You knew it, but misread the
question or made an arithmetic slip.

43
00:01:57,755 --> 00:01:59,791
Three, time.

44
00:02:00,076 --> 00:02:02,148
You rushed it or never got to it.

45
00:02:02,970 --> 00:02:04,720
Four, trap.

46
00:02:05,041 --> 00:02:08,184
You picked an answer that looked right
but didn't answer what was asked.

47
00:02:08,243 --> 00:02:09,243
Quick practice.

48
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:10,920
Here are three misses.

49
00:02:11,563 --> 00:02:12,993
Label each one.

50
00:02:13,635 --> 00:02:17,492
First, you solved for x, but the
question asked for x plus two.

51
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:22,170
Second, you knew the comma rule,
but ran out of time and guessed.

52
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:27,028
Third, you'd never learned how to get
a circle's radius from its equation.

53
00:02:27,112 --> 00:02:28,505
Pause and try it.

54
00:02:31,757 --> 00:02:33,293
The first is a trap.

55
00:02:33,686 --> 00:02:36,666
You did the math right and
answered the wrong question.

56
00:02:36,686 --> 00:02:38,507
The second is time.

57
00:02:38,971 --> 00:02:40,364
The third is content.

58
00:02:41,042 --> 00:02:42,971
And each one has a different fix.

59
00:02:43,971 --> 00:02:46,579
Drilling circle problems wouldn't
help the first two at all.

60
00:02:46,658 --> 00:02:48,086
Now count the labels.

61
00:02:48,407 --> 00:02:50,228
Content misses get drills.

62
00:02:50,265 --> 00:02:54,351
Careless misses get a habit, like
rereading what the question asks for before

63
00:02:54,371 --> 00:02:55,371
you answer.

64
00:02:55,444 --> 00:02:57,444
Time misses get pacing practice.

65
00:02:57,943 --> 00:03:01,551
Trap misses mean slowing
down on the last step.

66
00:03:01,729 --> 00:03:03,622
Your counts decide the rest of the week.

67
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:05,537
Days five and four are for drills.

68
00:03:05,930 --> 00:03:08,251
Pick the two skills where
you lost the most points.

69
00:03:08,680 --> 00:03:12,051
Then open the College Board's
free SAT Suite Question Bank.

70
00:03:12,071 --> 00:03:13,945
You can filter it by section, domain,

71
00:03:13,965 --> 00:03:15,480
skill, and difficulty.

72
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,445
Start at medium, and move up to hard
once most of the medium ones are going

73
00:03:18,465 --> 00:03:19,053
right.

74
00:03:19,073 --> 00:03:21,838
Do about fifteen to twenty
questions per skill,

75
00:03:21,858 --> 00:03:22,966
per day.

76
00:03:23,287 --> 00:03:25,731
Start untimed, so you
get the method right.

77
00:03:25,751 --> 00:03:28,787
Then do a second set on a clock.

78
00:03:29,073 --> 00:03:32,481
That's about seventy-one seconds a
question for Reading and Writing,

79
00:03:32,501 --> 00:03:34,608
and about ninety-five for math.

80
00:03:34,691 --> 00:03:37,334
When you miss one, add it
to your log with its label.

81
00:03:37,941 --> 00:03:41,084
By day four, you should see
fewer of the same mistake.

82
00:03:41,155 --> 00:03:44,027
If you don't, that's the
skill to keep drilling.

83
00:03:44,047 --> 00:03:45,762
Don't switch to something new.

84
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:47,625
One more job in this window.

85
00:03:48,267 --> 00:03:52,034
Exam setup opens in Bluebook one
to five days before your test,

86
00:03:52,054 --> 00:03:54,590
and you have to finish
it by the day before.

87
00:03:54,625 --> 00:03:57,248
Do it early, on the exact
device you're bringing.

88
00:03:57,268 --> 00:03:59,590
At the end, you get your admission ticket.

89
00:03:59,650 --> 00:04:02,006
Print the ticket, or email it to yourself.

90
00:04:02,077 --> 00:04:04,471
College Board says a
printed ticket is preferred.

91
00:04:04,614 --> 00:04:05,665
Then read it.

92
00:04:05,685 --> 00:04:07,879
It has your test center
and your arrival time,

93
00:04:07,899 --> 00:04:10,578
and sometimes special
instructions about your center.

94
00:04:10,627 --> 00:04:11,627
Day three.

95
00:04:11,913 --> 00:04:14,734
Keep drilling your weakest
skill, and add a Desmos warm-up.

96
00:04:15,555 --> 00:04:18,413
Bluebook has the Desmos
graphing calculator built in.

97
00:04:18,591 --> 00:04:20,376
The goal today is speed.

98
00:04:20,698 --> 00:04:23,590
You want the basic moves to
be automatic before test day.

99
00:04:23,648 --> 00:04:24,720
Try this one.

100
00:04:25,541 --> 00:04:30,200
What is the positive solution to the
equation three x squared minus five x minus

101
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:31,540
two equals zero?

102
00:04:31,605 --> 00:04:32,998
Pause and try it.

103
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:40,373
In Desmos, type y equals three x
squared minus five x minus two.

104
00:04:40,393 --> 00:04:45,016
Click the curve, and the points
where it crosses the x axis appear.

105
00:04:45,036 --> 00:04:51,551
One is negative one third, which Desmos
shows as negative zero point three three

106
00:04:51,571 --> 00:04:52,730
three.

107
00:04:52,750 --> 00:04:53,516
The other is two.

108
00:04:53,536 --> 00:04:55,143
So the positive solution is two.

109
00:04:55,181 --> 00:04:58,088
By hand, it factors into three x plus one,

110
00:04:58,108 --> 00:04:59,931
times x minus two.

111
00:05:00,573 --> 00:05:01,553
Same answer.

112
00:05:01,573 --> 00:05:03,661
Use whichever way is faster for you.

113
00:05:03,681 --> 00:05:06,788
And practice with the calculator
you'll use on test day.

114
00:05:07,001 --> 00:05:09,553
Bluebook's test preview
lets you try every tool,

115
00:05:09,573 --> 00:05:10,573
untimed.

116
00:05:10,639 --> 00:05:12,833
Also on day three, check your ID.

117
00:05:12,853 --> 00:05:15,726
It has to be a physical photo
ID, not a picture on your phone.

118
00:05:15,746 --> 00:05:16,976
It can't be expired.

119
00:05:16,996 --> 00:05:20,083
And the name has to match
your admission ticket exactly.

120
00:05:20,103 --> 00:05:22,582
If something's wrong,
you want to find out now,

121
00:05:22,602 --> 00:05:23,674
not at the door.

122
00:05:23,702 --> 00:05:24,702
Day two.

123
00:05:24,988 --> 00:05:26,167
Go lighter.

124
00:05:26,417 --> 00:05:28,397
Reread your mistake log, top to bottom.

125
00:05:28,417 --> 00:05:30,611
That log is the best study guide you have,

126
00:05:30,631 --> 00:05:32,631
because it's built from your own mistakes.

127
00:05:32,669 --> 00:05:34,578
Do a short mixed set,
maybe twenty minutes,

128
00:05:34,598 --> 00:05:36,027
just to stay sharp.

129
00:05:36,061 --> 00:05:38,435
Then look over the math reference
sheet so you know what's on it,

130
00:05:38,455 --> 00:05:39,598
and what isn't.

131
00:05:39,847 --> 00:05:41,705
Don't start any new topic.

132
00:05:41,811 --> 00:05:44,384
If it's brand new to you now,
it isn't worth the stress.

133
00:05:44,422 --> 00:05:45,636
Then logistics.

134
00:05:46,136 --> 00:05:49,438
Look up your test center and
how long it takes to get there.

135
00:05:49,458 --> 00:05:52,723
In the days before the test, College
Board says to keep checking that your test

136
00:05:52,743 --> 00:05:54,493
center hasn't changed or closed.

137
00:05:54,922 --> 00:05:57,115
Check your test card on
the Bluebook homepage,

138
00:05:57,135 --> 00:06:00,458
your email, and College Board's
test center closings page.

139
00:06:00,487 --> 00:06:04,380
Before the night before, here's
what not to do this week.

140
00:06:04,559 --> 00:06:06,166
Don't cram new content.

141
00:06:06,809 --> 00:06:09,736
And don't take another full
practice test in the last two days.

142
00:06:10,416 --> 00:06:14,523
It's tiring, and one off day can
shake your confidence for no reason.

143
00:06:14,565 --> 00:06:15,688
Don't change your sleep.

144
00:06:15,708 --> 00:06:18,366
Keep the same bedtime and
wake-up time all week.

145
00:06:18,386 --> 00:06:20,759
A late night, or a big
shift the night before,

146
00:06:20,779 --> 00:06:22,331
just leaves you tired.

147
00:06:22,351 --> 00:06:24,136
And don't switch calculators.

148
00:06:24,279 --> 00:06:28,188
If you bring your own, College
Board says to bring one you know.

149
00:06:28,208 --> 00:06:31,636
Otherwise, use the built-in
Desmos you've practiced with.

150
00:06:31,689 --> 00:06:33,597
Day one, the night before.

151
00:06:33,617 --> 00:06:37,883
First, if exam setup
isn't done, do it now.

152
00:06:37,903 --> 00:06:39,617
Today is the deadline.

153
00:06:39,653 --> 00:06:43,312
Then open Bluebook on your device, so you
know it starts up and is on the latest

154
00:06:43,332 --> 00:06:44,069
version.

155
00:06:44,089 --> 00:06:45,411
Now pack your bag.

156
00:06:45,838 --> 00:06:48,125
Your testing device, fully charged.

157
00:06:48,625 --> 00:06:51,553
College Board says it will need to
stay on for three to four hours.

158
00:06:52,053 --> 00:06:54,231
Your admission ticket, printed if you can.

159
00:06:54,696 --> 00:06:55,926
Your physical photo ID.

160
00:06:55,946 --> 00:06:58,017
Pencils or pens for scratch work.

161
00:06:58,303 --> 00:07:00,462
Your College Board username and password.

162
00:07:00,482 --> 00:07:03,126
And your calculator, if
you're bringing your own.

163
00:07:03,146 --> 00:07:05,947
Good to add: a charger
or portable battery,

164
00:07:05,967 --> 00:07:08,182
though College Board can't
promise you an outlet.

165
00:07:08,789 --> 00:07:12,304
A watch with no audible alarm, a
drink and a snack for the break,

166
00:07:12,324 --> 00:07:14,466
and a backup device if you have one.

167
00:07:14,824 --> 00:07:17,804
Staff will collect your phone or have
you store it away from your desk,

168
00:07:17,824 --> 00:07:19,789
so don't count on it for anything.

169
00:07:19,884 --> 00:07:21,741
Study for thirty minutes at most.

170
00:07:22,383 --> 00:07:25,027
Reread your log, then stop.

171
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:28,526
Lay out your clothes, set two alarms,

172
00:07:28,705 --> 00:07:30,955
and go to bed at your normal time.

173
00:07:31,205 --> 00:07:32,455
You've done the work.

174
00:07:33,347 --> 00:07:35,277
Tonight is about showing up rested.

175
00:07:35,345 --> 00:07:36,417
Test morning.

176
00:07:36,738 --> 00:07:39,218
Eat a normal breakfast,
the kind you usually eat.

177
00:07:39,238 --> 00:07:41,361
Check your bag one more time: ticket,

178
00:07:41,381 --> 00:07:43,147
ID, charged device.

179
00:07:43,167 --> 00:07:45,595
Then close and reopen Bluebook.

180
00:07:45,845 --> 00:07:49,254
College Board says to do that on test
day morning to check for last-minute

181
00:07:49,274 --> 00:07:51,738
messages and updated
test center information.

182
00:07:51,807 --> 00:07:54,286
Check your admission ticket
for your arrival time.

183
00:07:54,306 --> 00:07:56,394
Test centers open at 7:45 in the morning,

184
00:07:56,414 --> 00:07:59,413
and doors close at eight, unless
your ticket says otherwise.

185
00:08:00,199 --> 00:08:02,323
If you're borrowing a
device from College Board,

186
00:08:02,343 --> 00:08:04,450
arrive earlier, at 7:15.

187
00:08:04,485 --> 00:08:07,234
On the way, don't study.

188
00:08:07,271 --> 00:08:11,679
If you want a warm-up, do two or three
easy questions you've already solved,

189
00:08:11,699 --> 00:08:12,822
just to get your brain moving.

190
00:08:12,842 --> 00:08:15,699
Nothing new, and nothing hard.

191
00:08:15,749 --> 00:08:17,106
So here's the week.

192
00:08:17,678 --> 00:08:20,106
Day seven, a full timed test.

193
00:08:20,678 --> 00:08:23,105
Day six, label every miss.

194
00:08:23,606 --> 00:08:25,978
Days five and four, drill
your top two skills,

195
00:08:25,998 --> 00:08:27,499
and finish exam setup.

196
00:08:27,963 --> 00:08:30,749
Day three, Desmos and your ID.

197
00:08:30,999 --> 00:08:33,499
Day two, light review and logistics.

198
00:08:33,749 --> 00:08:36,142
Day one, pack and sleep.

199
00:08:36,570 --> 00:08:38,999
Then test morning, on time and calm.

200
00:08:39,067 --> 00:08:42,352
If your test isn't this week,
save this video for when it is.

201
00:08:42,459 --> 00:08:47,175
Next in this series: every SAT math
formula that's not on the reference sheet.

202
00:08:47,567 --> 00:08:49,603
If this helped, subscribe.

203
00:08:49,924 --> 00:08:50,924
And good luck.
